exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateActionPolicyOperationsDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Action Policy Operations Schema Reference

#### \`operation: \\"set_metadata\\"\`

Use \`set_metadata\` to name the action policy and add a description.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_metadata\\" | required |  |
| \`name\` | string | optional | The action policy name. (min length: 1, max length: 256) |
| \`description\` | string | optional | A description of the action policy. (max length: 1024) |

#### \`operation: \\"set_destinations\\"\`

Use \`set_destinations\` to choose which workflows receive notifications (email, Slack, PagerDuty, etc.).

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_destinations\\" | required |  |
| \`destinations\` | { type: \\"workflow\\", ... }[] | required | The list of workflow destinations. (min items: 1, max items: 10) |

#### \`operation: \\"set_matcher\\"\`

Use \`set_matcher\` to limit which alert episodes this policy notifies on. An empty or null matcher matches all episodes in the space.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_matcher\\" | required |  |
| \`matcher\` | object \\\\| null | required | Structured matcher for alert episodes, or null for a catch-all. |

#### \`operation: \\"set_grouping\\"\`

Use \`set_grouping\` to batch matched episodes into notifications — one per episode, one for all matching episodes, or grouped by field.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_grouping\\" | required |  |
| \`groupingMode\` | \\"per_episode\\" \\\\| \\"all\\" \\\\| \\"per_field\\" | optional | The grouping mode. |
| \`groupBy\` | string[] \\\\| null | optional | Fields used to group alerts (required when groupingMode is per_field). |

#### \`operation: \\"set_throttle\\"\`

Use \`set_throttle\` to limit how often notifications fire so the user is not flooded by repeat alerts.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_throttle\\" | required |  |
| \`strategy\` | \\"on_status_change\\" \\\\| \\"per_status_interval\\" \\\\| \\"time_interval\\" \\\\| \\"every_time\\" | optional | The throttle strategy. |
| \`interval\` | string | optional | The throttle interval (e.g. 5m, 1h). (max length: 32) |

#### \`operation: \\"validate\\"\`

Use \`validate\` as the last operation to confirm the action policy is complete and ready to save.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"validate\\" | required |  |"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateActionPolicySchemaDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Action Policy API Schema Reference

## Top-Level Fields

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`name\` | string | required | The name of the action policy. (min length: 1, max length: 256) |
| \`description\` | string | required | A description of the action policy. (max length: 1024) |
| \`destinations\` | { type: \\"workflow\\", ... }[] | required | The list of destinations. At least one is required. (min items: 1, max items: 10) |
| \`matcher\` | object | optional | Selects the alerts this policy applies to. Set \`tags\` to match alerts from rules with those tags. Set \`expression\` to a KQL query, which will be evaluated against each alert. <br/><br/> If you set both \`tags\` and \`expression\`, an alert must match the tags and the expression for the policy to apply. When \`matcher\` is \`null\`, or when both \`tags\` and \`expression\` are empty, the policy applies to all alerts. |
| \`group_by\` | string[] | optional | The fields used to group alerts. (max items: 16) |
| \`grouping_mode\` | \\"per_episode\\" \\\\| \\"all\\" \\\\| \\"per_field\\" | optional | The grouping mode for alert notifications. |
| \`throttle\` | object | optional | The throttle configuration for notifications. |"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateActionPolicyWorkflowPayloadDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Action Policy Workflow Dispatch Payload

Catalog of fields the dispatcher passes as \`inputs.payload\`. In Liquid:
\`{{ inputs.payload.<field> }}\`, and inside
\`{% for ep in inputs.payload.episodes %}\` use \`{{ ep.<field> }}\`.

## Top-Level Fields (\`inputs.payload\`)

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`id\` | string | required | Unique identifier of the notification group |
| \`policyId\` | string | required | Identifier of the notification policy that matched and dispatched the group |
| \`groupKey\` | object | required | Grouping key for the notification group. This is currently generated by the dispatcher and may vary depending on grouping behavior. |
| \`episodes\` | object[] | required | Alert episodes included in this notification group |
| \`rules\` | object | required | Rule metadata keyed by rule id. Each value contains rule metadata (e.g. \`{ name }\`) for a rule referenced by the episodes. Access via \`rules[episode.rule_id].name\`. |

## Episode Fields (\`inputs.payload.episodes[]\`)

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`last_event_timestamp\` | string | required | Timestamp of the latest event seen for this episode |
| \`rule_id\` | string \\\\| null | required | Identifier of the rule that produced this episode |
| \`source\` | string | required | Origin of the alert events for this episode (e.g. \`internal\`) |
| \`space_id\` | string | required | Identifier of the space the episode belongs to |
| \`group_hash\` | string | required | Hash identifying the alert series/group this episode belongs to |
| \`episode_id\` | string | required | Unique identifier of the alert episode |
| \`episode_status\` | \\"inactive\\" \\\\| \\"pending\\" \\\\| \\"active\\" \\\\| \\"recovering\\" | required | Current lifecycle status of the alert episode (enum: inactive \\\\| pending \\\\| active \\\\| recovering) |
| \`severity\` | \\"info\\" \\\\| \\"low\\" \\\\| \\"medium\\" \\\\| \\"high\\" \\\\| \\"critical\\" | optional | Severity of the alert episode (enum: info \\\\| low \\\\| medium \\\\| high \\\\| critical) |
| \`data\` | object | optional | Data of the alert episode |

### \`data\`

\`data\` is the rule's ES|QL result row (each query row is written as \`data: rowDoc\`
on the alert event). Columns depend on the rule query, so they are not listed above.

- Nested dotted names: \`ep.data.host.name\`, not \`ep.data[\\"host.name\\"]\`.
- Discover columns with \`| LIMIT 0\` if they are unclear.
- Guard empty \`data\` on recovering/inactive: \`| default\` or \`{% if ep.data %}\`.

## Example

For \`FROM logs-* | STATS error_count = COUNT(*) BY host.name | WHERE error_count >= 5\`:

\`\`\`yaml
version: '1'
name: \\"Notify: <rule-name>\\"
enabled: true
triggers:
  - type: manual
steps:
  - name: send_email
    type: email
    connector-id: <connector-id>
    with:
      to:
        - <user-provided-email>
      subject: \\"Alert: {{ inputs.payload.episodes | size }} episode(s)\\"
      message: >
        {% for ep in inputs.payload.episodes %}
        - Rule: {{ inputs.payload.rules[ep.rule_id].name | default: \\"unknown\\" }}
          Host: {{ ep.data.host.name | default: \\"unknown\\" }}
          Errors: {{ ep.data.error_count | default: \\"n/a\\" }}
          Status: {{ ep.episode_status }}
        {% endfor %}

        View execution: {{ execution.url }}
\`\`\`"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateDispatchFlowDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Dispatch Flow

The end-to-end notification path:

1. **Rule** (\`kind: alert\`) evaluates its ES|QL query and writes alert episodes to \`.rule-events\`.
2. **Dispatcher** (runs on its own Task Manager schedule) reads episodes from \`.rule-events\`.
3. Dispatcher loads **enabled action policies** for the relevant space.
4. **Matcher evaluation**: each policy's KQL matcher is tested against each episode's context.
5. **Grouping**: matched episodes are grouped according to the policy's \`groupingMode\` / \`groupBy\`.
6. **Throttling**: groups are filtered based on the policy's throttle strategy and notification history.
7. **Dispatch**: eligible groups are sent to the policy's **workflow destinations** via \`scheduleWorkflow\`.
8. **Workflow execution**: workflow steps run, using connectors to deliver notifications (email, Slack, etc.).

Signal rules (\`kind: signal\`) are excluded at step 2 — the dispatcher query only selects \`type == 'alert'\` events."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateEpisodeLifecycleDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Episode Lifecycle

Episodes are the unit of alert state. Each unique group (by \`group_hash\`) has its own episode. Each episode has a status that reflects where it is in the lifecycle:

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| \`inactive\` | The alert episode is fully recovered |
| \`pending\` | Breached but below the consecutive-breaches threshold |
| \`active\` | Met the threshold — alert is firing |
| \`recovering\` | Breach stopped but recovery condition is not yet met |

Only \`kind: alert\` rules produce episodes. \`kind: signal\` rules write raw signal events with no episode tracking."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateGroupingModesDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Grouping Modes

- \`per_episode\`: one notification per alert episode lifecycle (default).
- \`all\`: a single notification for all matching episodes.
- \`per_field\`: group by specified \`groupBy\` fields.

Throttle strategy must be compatible with the grouping mode — see [action-policy-throttle-grouping-compatibility](./action-policy-throttle-grouping-compatibility.md)."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateMatcherContextDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Matcher Context Fields

When the dispatcher evaluates a policy's KQL matcher, these fields are available:

| Field | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| \`episode_id\` | string | The episode UUID |
| \`episode_status\` | \`inactive\`, \`pending\`, \`active\`, \`recovering\` | Episode lifecycle status |
| \`group_hash\` | string | Hash of the grouping fields |
| \`last_event_timestamp\` | string | Timestamp of the most recent event |
| \`severity\` | \`info\`, \`low\`, \`medium\`, \`high\`, \`critical\` | Episode severity when present |
| \`rule.id\` | string | The rule's saved object ID |
| \`rule.name\` | string | The rule's display name |
| \`rule.tags\` | string[] | The rule's tags array |
| \`data\` | \`data.*\` object | Rule-specific ES\\\\|QL output columns (query as \`data.*\`, e.g. \`data.host.name\`, \`data.error_count\`) |

An empty matcher is a catch-all that matches all episodes in the space. To scope a policy to a single rule, use \`rule.id: \\"<ruleId>\\"\`."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateMultiRuleActionPolicyDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Multi-rule Action Policies

Use this path when the user wants **one policy across several rules**, a space-wide
catch-all, or routing by tag/severity — not a policy tied to a single \`rule.id\`.

Create the policy with \`set_metadata\` (name by intent, not by one rule),
\`set_destinations\` (same \`workflowId\` rule as the single-rule path), a matcher from
the options below, then \`set_grouping\` / \`set_throttle\`.

A policy matches **episodes**, not a rule object. Policies are space-scoped and are
not bound to a single rule. The matcher is optional KQL over
[matcher context fields](./action-policy-matchers.md).

- **Catch-all**: omit \`set_matcher\` or set matcher to empty/\`null\`. Confirm with the
  user first — this notifies on every \`kind: alert\` episode in the space, including
  rules created later.
- **Several specific rules**: \`rule.id: \\"<id1>\\" or rule.id: \\"<id2>\\"\`. Collect each
  \`ruleId\` from \`manage_rule\` / \`sml_search\` (pre-assigned IDs work for unsaved drafts).
- **A family of rules**: \`rule.tags: \\"production\\"\` or \`rule.name: \\"CPU*\\"\`. Prefer tags
  when the set will grow.
- **Route by severity across rules**: \`severity: \\"critical\\"\` (or combine with tags).
  Useful for a PagerDuty policy vs an email policy.
- **Reuse destinations**: one workflow can serve many rules. Keep Liquid generic —
  \`inputs.payload.rules[ep.rule_id].name\`, \`ep.episode_status\`, and guarded
  \`ep.data.*\` — because query columns often differ across rules. If two rules need
  different message shapes, use two policies (or two workflows) rather than one
  brittle template. See [workflow-dispatch-payload](./workflow-dispatch-payload.md).
- **Search first**: run \`platform.core.sml_search\` for existing policies before adding
  another catch-all or overlapping tag matcher.
- **Grouping**: \`per_episode\` is still a safe default. \`all\` batches mixed-rule
  episodes into a single notification; only use it when the user wants one combined
  message.

Name shared policies by intent (\`\\"Notify production alerts\\"\`, \`\\"Page on critical\\"\`),
not by a single rule name.

For the default one-rule path, see [single-rule action policies](./action-policy-single-rule.md)."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateNoDataStrategyDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# No-Data Strategy

\`no_data\` is a **top-level rule field** that controls what happens when the rule finds no data for a group. It is set on \`set_query\` as an object whose \`strategy\` selects the behaviour. Omitting it on an alert rule stores \`{ strategy: 'ignore' }\`.

| Strategy | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| \`ignore\` | Never checks for presence. Runs where a group is absent are not classified. |
| \`keep_last\` | Keeps the episode's previous status when the rule finds no data. |
| \`resolve\` | Marks the episode \`inactive\` the first time the rule finds no data. |
| \`alert\` | Marks the episode \`active\` when the rule finds no data. |

Every strategy except \`ignore\` may carry its own presence query:
\`no_data: { strategy: 'keep_last', query: 'FROM heartbeat-* | STATS count = COUNT(*) BY host.name | WHERE count >= 1' }\`.
Omit \`query\` to use \`query.base\` to decide whether a group has data.

Signal rules cannot set \`no_data\` ([rule-kind reference](./rule-kind.md))."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateNotificationsOverviewDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Notifications via Action Policies

Notifications are not configured on the rule itself. Alert episodes are matched and dispatched by **action policies** — space-scoped saved objects that send matched episodes to workflow destinations.

When the user needs notifications (email, Slack, PagerDuty, etc.), load the \`action-policy-management\` skill. That skill owns action policy CRUD, workflow destination wiring, and the default notification setup flow.

## Notifications Require Alert Kind

Action policies only process Alerts (\`kind: alert\`). Events (\`kind: signal\`) do not participate in episode lifecycle or notification dispatch. See the [rule-kind reference](./rule-kind.md) and [episode-lifecycle reference](./episode-lifecycle.md).

When a user asks for notifications on a rule that is currently \`kind: signal\` (or when composing a new rule where the user wants notifications):

1. **Explain the difference**: Events (\`kind: signal\`) rules are observation-only and do not trigger notifications. Alerts (\`kind: alert\`) track episode lifecycle and can dispatch to action policies.
2. If the rule is a **draft (in-memory)**: use \`set_kind\` to change it to \`alert\`, then load the \`action-policy-management\` skill for notification setup.
3. If the rule is **persisted**: \`kind\` is immutable after creation. Inform the user that the existing Events (\`kind: signal\`) rule cannot be converted. Offer to create a new Alerts (\`kind: alert\`) rule with the same query and schedule, then set up notifications on the new rule.
4. After ensuring the rule is \`kind: alert\`, load the \`action-policy-management\` skill for notification setup."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateRecoveryStrategyDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Recovery Strategy

\`recovery\` is a **top-level rule field** (not inside the query). It is set on \`set_query\` as an object whose \`strategy\` selects the behaviour, and it controls how episodes transition from active to recovering/inactive (see [episode-lifecycle reference](./episode-lifecycle.md)). Omitting it on an alert rule stores \`{ strategy: 'no_breach' }\`. Signal rules (\`kind: signal\`) cannot set \`recovery\` ([rule-kind reference](./rule-kind.md)).

- \`no_breach\`: Recovers a group when it stops appearing in the breach results.
- \`condition\`: Recovers a group when \`query.base\` plus this segment returns it. Requires \`query.breach\`.
- \`query\`: Recovers a group when this independent query returns it.
- \`manual\`: Never recovers automatically. Only user actions close the episode.

The two query-backed strategies carry their own ES|QL:
- \`recovery: { strategy: 'condition', segment: 'WHERE avg_cpu < 0.6' }\` — the segment is appended to \`query.base\`. This requires \`query.breach\`: without a breach segment every row of \`base\` already breaches, so the recovery condition could only return groups that are breaching and the rule would never recover.
- \`recovery: { strategy: 'query', query: 'FROM metrics-* | STATS avg_cpu = AVG(cpu) BY host.name | WHERE avg_cpu < 0.6' }\` — an independent full query, usable with or without \`query.breach\`."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateRuleKindDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Rule Kind: Alerts vs Events

Rules declare a \`kind\` of \`alert\` (Alerts) or \`signal\` (Events). This is the most important behavioral split in the system.

### Alerts (\`kind: alert\`)
Creates an alert for each matching group and tracks it until it recovers. Use this when you want to detect a problem and notify or automate a response.
Episode statuses: \`inactive\`, \`pending\`, \`active\`, \`recovering\`.
State transition fields: \`pending\`, \`recovering\`.

### Events (\`kind: signal\`)
Stores each match as a rule event you can query. Alerts are not created and notifications are not sent.

## Immutability
\`kind\` is **immutable on persisted rules** — it can only be set at creation time. The update API rejects changes to \`kind\`. For draft (in-memory) rules, \`set_kind\` can change it freely."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateRuleOperationsDoc matches the snapshot 1`] = `
"# Rule Operations Schema Reference

#### \`operation: \\"set_metadata\\"\`

Use \`set_metadata\` to name the rule and add a description or tags so the user can filter by it later.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`name\` | string | optional | Rule name (must be unique within the space). (min length: 1, max length: 256) |
| \`description\` | string | optional | Human-readable description of the rule. (max length: 1024) |
| \`tags\` | string[] | optional | Tags for categorization, e.g. [\\"production\\", \\"infra\\"]. (min items: 1, max items: 20) |
| \`builder_type\` | string | optional | Identifies the rule builder that authored this rule (e.g. \\"threshold\\"). Absent for rules authored directly in ES\\\\|QL. (max length: 64) |
| \`operation\` | \\"set_metadata\\" | required |  |

#### \`operation: \\"set_kind\\"\`

Use \`set_kind\` to choose a rule kind matching the user's goal: detect and respond (\`alert\`) or collect evidence (\`signal\`).

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_kind\\" | required |  |
| \`kind\` | \\"alert\\" \\\\| \\"signal\\" | required | Whether the rule creates alerts (\`alert\`) or only stores matching events (\`signal\`). |

#### \`operation: \\"set_schedule\\"\`

Use \`set_schedule\` to control how often the rule runs (\`every\`) and how far back each run looks (\`lookback\`).

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`every\` | string | optional | Execution interval, e.g. 1m, 5m, 1h. (max length: 32) |
| \`lookback\` | string | optional | Lookback window for the query, e.g. 5m, 1h. Can also be expressed in ES\\\\|QL. (max length: 32) |
| \`operation\` | \\"set_schedule\\" | required |  |

#### \`operation: \\"set_query\\"\`

Use \`set_query\` to define the ES|QL condition that should fire the rule. Optionally set how recovery is detected and what happens when data stops arriving.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_query\\" | required |  |
| \`query\` | object | required | Detection query configuration. |
| \`recovery\` | { strategy: \\"no_breach\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"condition\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"query\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"manual\\", ... } | optional | How an alert recovers. Required when \`kind\` is \`alert\`; defaults to \`no_breach\` when omitted. Not allowed when \`kind\` is \`signal\`. |
| \`no_data\` | { strategy: \\"ignore\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"keep_last\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"resolve\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"alert\\", ... } | optional | What the rule does when it finds no data for a group. Required when \`kind\` is \`alert\`; defaults to \`ignore\` when omitted. Not allowed when \`kind\` is \`signal\`. |

##### \`query\`

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`base\` | string | required | The detection query, and the only place a \`FROM\` lives. Time filters are applied automatically via the lookback window. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |
| \`breach\` | object | optional | Breach condition appended to \`base\`. Omit to treat every row returned by \`base\` as a breach. |

#### \`operation: \\"set_grouping\\"\`

Use \`set_grouping\` to split alerts by entity (host, service, etc.) so each group has its own episode instead of one combined alert.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`fields\` | string[] | required | Fields to group alerts by, e.g. [\\"host.name\\", \\"service.name\\"]. Should match ES\\\\|QL GROUP BY fields. (max items: 16) |
| \`operation\` | \\"set_grouping\\" | required |  |

#### \`operation: \\"set_state_transition\\"\`

Use \`set_state_transition\` to delay alert firing until the threshold is breached N times in a row. This reduces noise from transient spikes. State transition is only allowed on \`kind: alert\` rules.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`pending\` | object | optional | Gating for the \`breached\` → \`active\` transition. |
| \`recovering\` | object | optional | Gating for the \`recovered\` → \`inactive\` transition. |
| \`operation\` | \\"set_state_transition\\" | required |  |

##### \`pending\`

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`count\` | integer | optional | Number of consecutive matches required before the alert becomes \`active\`. \`0\` skips the \`pending\` phase. (min: 0, max: 1000) |
| \`timeframe\` | string | optional | Time window used with \`count\`, for example \`5m\` or \`15m\`. (max length: 32) |
| \`operator\` | \\"AND\\" \\\\| \\"OR\\" | optional | The operator that combines \`count\` and \`timeframe\`. \`AND\` requires both, \`OR\` requires either. Only allowed when both are set. (enum: AND \\\\| OR) |

##### \`recovering\`

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`count\` | integer | optional | Number of consecutive recoveries required before the alert becomes \`inactive\`. \`0\` skips the \`recovering\` phase. (min: 0, max: 1000) |
| \`timeframe\` | string | optional | Time window used with \`count\`, for example \`5m\` or \`15m\`. (max length: 32) |
| \`operator\` | \\"AND\\" \\\\| \\"OR\\" | optional | The operator that combines \`count\` and \`timeframe\`. \`AND\` requires both, \`OR\` requires either. Only allowed when both are set. (enum: AND \\\\| OR) |

#### \`operation: \\"set_dashboards\\"\`

Use \`set_dashboards\` to link investigation dashboards to the rule by saved-object ID. Each ID is stored as a \`dashboard\` artifact (\`{ id, type: \\"dashboard\\", data: { dashboard_id } }\`), matching the create/update API. Replaces any previously linked dashboards; other artifacts (e.g. runbooks) are preserved. Pass an empty array to unlink all dashboards.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_dashboards\\" | required |  |
| \`dashboard_ids\` | string[] | required | Dashboard saved-object IDs to link as investigation artifacts on the rule. Each ID must be an existing dashboard the current user can read. Replaces previously linked dashboards. Pass an empty array to unlink all dashboards. (max items: 100) |

#### \`operation: \\"set_runbook\\"\`

Use \`set_runbook\` to attach investigation markdown to the rule. Stored as a \`runbook\` artifact (\`{ id, type: \\"runbook\\", data: { content } }\`), matching the create/update API. Replaces any previously attached runbook; other artifacts (e.g. dashboards) are preserved. Pass \`null\` or an empty string to unlink. Whitespace-only content is treated as unlink. Content longer than 50000 characters is rejected.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"set_runbook\\" | required |  |
| \`content\` | string \\\\| null | required | Markdown investigation steps. Stored as a \`runbook\` artifact (\`{ id, type: \\"runbook\\", data: { content } }\`). Replaces any previously attached runbook; other artifacts (e.g. dashboards) are preserved. Pass \`null\` or an empty string to unlink. Whitespace-only content is treated as unlink. Max 50000 characters. |

#### \`operation: \\"validate\\"\`

Use \`validate\` as the last operation to confirm the rule is complete and ready to save.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`operation\` | \\"validate\\" | required |  |"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateRuleSchemaDoc matches the snapshot 1`] = `
"# Rule API Schema Reference

## Top-Level Fields

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`kind\` | \\"alert\\" \\\\| \\"signal\\" | required | Whether the rule creates alerts (\`alert\`) or only stores matching events (\`signal\`). |
| \`metadata\` | object | required | Rule metadata. |
| \`time_field\` | string | required | Document field used as the event time when applying the lookback window. Defaults to \`@timestamp\`. (min length: 1, max length: 128, default: \\"@timestamp\\") |
| \`schedule\` | object | required | Execution schedule configuration. |
| \`query\` | object | required | Detection query configuration. |
| \`recovery\` | { strategy: \\"no_breach\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"condition\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"query\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"manual\\", ... } | optional | How an alert recovers. Required when \`kind\` is \`alert\`; defaults to \`no_breach\` when omitted. Not allowed when \`kind\` is \`signal\`. |
| \`no_data\` | { strategy: \\"ignore\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"keep_last\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"resolve\\", ... } \\\\| { strategy: \\"alert\\", ... } | optional | What the rule does when it finds no data for a group. Required when \`kind\` is \`alert\`; defaults to \`ignore\` when omitted. Not allowed when \`kind\` is \`signal\`. |
| \`state_transition\` | object \\\\| null | optional |  |
| \`grouping\` | object | optional | Grouping configuration. |
| \`artifacts\` | object[] | optional | Optional objects attached to the rule, such as a runbook or a dashboard. Each item has \`id\`, \`type\`, and \`data\`. The shape of \`data\` depends on \`type\`. For example, a \`runbook\` uses \`content\` and a \`dashboard\` uses \`dashboard_id\`. Known types are validated against that shape. Unknown types are stored when \`id\`, \`type\`, and \`data\` are present. (max items: 100) |

## Query

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`base\` | string | required | The detection query, and the only place a \`FROM\` lives. Time filters are applied automatically via the lookback window. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |
| \`breach\` | object | optional | Breach condition appended to \`base\`. Omit to treat every row returned by \`base\` as a breach. |

#### \`breach\`

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`segment\` | string | required | A clause appended to \`query.base\`, for example \`WHERE avg_cpu > 0.85\`. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |

## Recovery Strategies

#### \`strategy: \\"no_breach\\"\`

Recovers a group when it stops appearing in the breach results.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"no_breach\\" | required |  |

#### \`strategy: \\"condition\\"\`

Recovers a group when \`query.base\` plus this segment returns it. Requires \`query.breach\`.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"condition\\" | required |  |
| \`segment\` | string | required | A clause appended to \`query.base\`, for example \`WHERE avg_cpu < 0.60\`. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |

#### \`strategy: \\"query\\"\`

Recovers a group when this independent query returns it.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"query\\" | required |  |
| \`query\` | string | required | Full ES\\\\|QL query for recovery detection. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |

#### \`strategy: \\"manual\\"\`

Never recovers automatically. Only user actions close the episode.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"manual\\" | required |  |

## No-Data Strategies

#### \`strategy: \\"ignore\\"\`

Never checks for presence. Runs where a group is absent are not classified.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"ignore\\" | required |  |

#### \`strategy: \\"keep_last\\"\`

Keeps the episode's previous status when the rule finds no data.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"keep_last\\" | required |  |
| \`query\` | string | optional | Presence query. When omitted, \`query.base\` decides whether a group has data. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |

#### \`strategy: \\"resolve\\"\`

Marks the episode \`inactive\` the first time the rule finds no data.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"resolve\\" | required |  |
| \`query\` | string | optional | Presence query. When omitted, \`query.base\` decides whether a group has data. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |

#### \`strategy: \\"alert\\"\`

Marks the episode \`active\` when the rule finds no data.

| Field | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`strategy\` | \\"alert\\" | required |  |
| \`query\` | string | optional | Presence query. When omitted, \`query.base\` decides whether a group has data. (min length: 1, max length: 10000) |"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateSeverityDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Alert Event Severity

Severity is a per-event property on alert events and episodes, not a rule-level field. It is extracted at execution time from a column named \`severity\` in the ES|QL breach query output.

- **Valid values**: \`info\`, \`low\`, \`medium\`, \`high\`, \`critical\` (case-insensitive).
- If the breach query does not produce a \`severity\` column, alert events have no severity.
- Different groups can produce different severities in the same rule execution (the value comes from each row).
- Action policies can match on \`severity\` to route high-severity episodes differently (e.g. PagerDuty for critical, email for low).

### Setting Severity in ES|QL

Severity is set by adding a \`severity\` column to the breach query via \`EVAL\`:

- **Literal severity** — all alerts from the rule share the same severity:
  \`| EVAL severity = \\"critical\\"\`
- **Conditional severity** — severity varies per group based on data:
  \`| EVAL severity = CASE(cpu > 0.95, \\"critical\\", cpu > 0.8, \\"high\\", \\"medium\\")\`"
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateSingleRuleActionPolicyDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Single-rule Action Policies

Use this path when the user wants notifications for **one specific rule**.

Action policies only process alert episodes. If the rule is \`kind: signal\`, do not
proceed: ask the user (or the rule-management skill) to convert or recreate the
rule as \`kind: alert\` first.

Create the policy with these operations in order:

1. \`set_metadata\`: name = \`\\"Notify on <rule-name>\\"\`, description = \`\\"Default notification for <rule-name>\\"\`
2. \`set_destinations\`: \`[{ type: \\"workflow\\", id: \\"<workflowId>\\" }]\`
   - Use the \`workflowId\` passed to \`generate_workflow\`, **not** the workflow \`attachmentId\`.
3. \`set_matcher\`: \`rule.id: \\"<ruleId>\\"\`
   - \`ruleId\` comes from the \`manage_rule\` tool result. It is pre-assigned when the
     rule attachment is created and becomes the saved-object ID when the user clicks
     \\"Create rule\\".
   - It is available even for unsaved/proposed rules — do not ask the user to save
     the rule first.
4. \`set_grouping\`: \`per_episode\`
5. \`set_throttle\`: \`{ strategy: \\"on_status_change\\" }\`

If the user explicitly requests a cross-rule or shared policy, consult the
[multi-rule action policies reference](./action-policy-multi-rule.md)."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateStateTransitionDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"## State Transition

Use \`set_state_transition\` to delay alert firing until the threshold is breached N times in a row. This reduces noise from transient spikes.

- \`pending\` — Gating for the \`breached\` → \`active\` transition.
  - \`pending.count\` — Number of consecutive matches required before the alert becomes \`active\`. \`0\` skips the \`pending\` phase.
  - \`pending.timeframe\` — Time window used with \`count\`, for example \`5m\` or \`15m\`.
  - \`pending.operator\` — The operator that combines \`count\` and \`timeframe\`. \`AND\` requires both, \`OR\` requires either. Only allowed when both are set.
- \`recovering\` — Gating for the \`recovered\` → \`inactive\` transition.
  - \`recovering.count\` — Number of consecutive recoveries required before the alert becomes \`inactive\`. \`0\` skips the \`recovering\` phase.
  - \`recovering.timeframe\` — Time window used with \`count\`, for example \`5m\` or \`15m\`.
  - \`recovering.operator\` — The operator that combines \`count\` and \`timeframe\`. \`AND\` requires both, \`OR\` requires either. Only allowed when both are set.

State transition is only allowed on \`kind: alert\` rules. Refer to the [rule-operations-schema reference](./references/rule-operations-schema.md) for the full field schema."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateThrottleGroupingCompatibilityDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Throttle / Grouping Compatibility

- \`per_episode\`: one notification per alert episode lifecycle (default).
- \`all\`: a single notification for all matching episodes.
- \`per_field\`: group by specified \`groupBy\` fields.

Caveats:
- Only valid with \`per_episode\`: \`on_status_change\`, \`per_status_interval\`.
- Not valid with \`per_episode\`: \`time_interval\`.
- Require an \`interval\` (e.g. \`\\"5m\\"\`, \`\\"1h\\"\`): \`per_status_interval\`, \`time_interval\`.

If you set both in one request, put \`set_grouping\` before \`set_throttle\`. The tool
validates compatibility after all operations run.

Related: [action-policy-grouping-modes](./action-policy-grouping-modes.md), [action-policy-throttle-strategies](./action-policy-throttle-strategies.md)."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateThrottleStrategiesDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Throttle Strategies

- \`on_status_change\`: notify only on episode status transitions (default for \`per_episode\`).
- \`per_status_interval\`: notify on transitions and at regular intervals.
- \`time_interval\`: notify at regular intervals regardless of status (default for \`all\`/\`per_field\`).
- \`every_time\`: notify on every evaluation cycle (high volume).

Compatibility with grouping modes — see [action-policy-throttle-grouping-compatibility](./action-policy-throttle-grouping-compatibility.md)."
`;

exports[`schema_to_skill_docs generateWorkflowDestinationsDoc matches the reviewed skill-doc snapshot 1`] = `
"# Workflows

A workflow is a **concrete automation defined in YAML** that executes when dispatched by an action policy.

- Workflow steps can use Kibana **connectors** (email, Slack, PagerDuty, etc.) via the \`connector-id\` field on each step.
- Action policy destinations reference **workflow IDs**, never connector IDs directly.
- Destination workflows must use **exactly one** \`triggers: - type: manual\` trigger — never \`alert\`.
- For deeper connector knowledge (types, \`connector-id\` usage, discovery tools), load the \`workflow-authoring\` skill."
`;
